  "image_name": "0deeb92e9410ac9ee06fa91d3c9e5523.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how endothelial cells are spatially specialized in the kidney and liver, and how disease changes their dominant pathways.\n\nIn the kidney, a schematic nephron is overlaid on cortical, glomerular, and medullary regions. Each vascular niche is paired with example marker genes and transcription factors, emphasizing that endothelial cells differ strongly by location. The cortex, glomerulus, and medulla are shown as distinct compartments linked to different injury contexts. On the right, disease-associated shifts are summarized: immune injury and diabetes in cortical or glomerular regions are associated with increased angiogenesis, while diabetes also increases glucose transport. In the medulla, dehydration is linked to higher oxidative phosphorylation, hypoxia-related programs, and glycolysis.\n\nIn the liver, the diagram shows zonated liver sinusoidal endothelial cells arranged from Zone 1 to Zone 3 across the lobule, plus a separate portal endothelial population. Each zone has its own marker set, indicating graded specialization along the porto-central axis. Disease effects are again summarized at right: hepatocellular carcinoma appears to disrupt Zone 3 features, reducing capillarization, endocytosis, and vascular tone, while cirrhosis is associated with more extracellular matrix, loss of sinusoidal endothelial identity, and reduced permeability.\n\nOverall, the image highlights organ- and region-specific endothelial identities and their selective remodeling in disease.",
